                <pb facs="0225" n="149" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg149"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">660F.116/166: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Czechoslovakia</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Wright</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-06-18T15:00:00-04:00">June 18, 1935—3 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>17. The Department’s mail instruction 49, May 25. Packing interests continue to
                    complain against discriminatory treatment in respect of lard. We do not seem to
                    have received information with regard to the quota allocation for June, and
                    unless those allocations involve a proportionate increase for the United States,
                    I feel that the matter should be taken up with the Czech Government with a view
                    to impressing upon it the fact that the <hi rendition="#italic">modus
                        vivendi</hi> which came into effect May 1 places on that Government an
                    obligation to accord American interests more favorable treatment than has been
                    accorded in the past.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
